{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/spec.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/architecture.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/behavior.md'","aggregated_output":"Purpose: section rules for `specs/<id>/spec.md`. Template: `assets/spec.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `id` as YYYY-MM-DD-slug; `kind`, feature or fix; `status`, draft, building or shipped; `summary`, one user-facing line reused in release notes; `request`, the sentence, issue or commits it started from; `issue`, optional, delete the line when there is none. The owner's go is `approved: {by: <owner>, via: <in session, a message link, or a PR>}`, added only when the user reports it, never inferred; git dates it. After a change to Why or What changes, remove the line; the owner's new go writes it back. The go is never a status.\n\nWhy: required. The problem, who has it, and what it costs to leave it, in 2 to 5 lines. No solution.\n\nWhat changes: required. Claims under `### Add · <domain>/<capability>`, `### Change · ...` and `### Remove · ...`, only the headings that apply, one per capability. A Change claim carries a `was:` line with the current text on main. A Remove claim carries a `reason:` line. A fix that changes no behavior writes \"No behavior change.\" and nothing else here. Claims read exactly as they will in behavior/.\n\nNot doing: required for a feature, optional for a fix. What is deliberately out, so scope can't creep silently. Not future ideas with no decision behind them.\n\nOpen questions: while drafting, only questions the owner can answer. Delete the section when empty; the owner can't say go before that. Questions the agent can answer from the code never go here.\n\nA spec past about 80 lines is two specs.\nPurpose: rules for `architecture/<area>.md`. Templates: `assets/architecture/<area>.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `area`, the area name. A title, then the area's own table or list. Current state only: no history, no plans. The why links to decision records.\n\nArea files are created the first time they have content, never empty. `stack.md` exists from init, with the commands spec-build and spec-ship run. The folder listing is its own index.\n\n| Area | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `stack` | languages and frameworks, and the commands to install, run, test, lint and build |\n| `conventions` | only the rules agents get wrong without being told: naming, error handling, test style |\n| `boundaries` | modules: name, path, domain, what each may depend on |\n| `data-model` | entities: name, key fields, relations |\n| `contracts` | APIs and events: name, kind, input, output, consumers |\n| `integrations` | external services: purpose, direction, auth, what the product does when it fails |\n| `config` | settings and flags: name, purpose, default, where it's set; never real secret values |\n| `invariants` | non-negotiable rules every spec must keep: ID, rule, why |\n\nWritten by docs-map for what a domain needs, and by spec-ship for the files plan.md lists under Architecture to update at ship.\nPurpose: rules for `behavior/<domain>/<capability>.md`. Template: `assets/behavior-capability.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `capability`, as `<domain>/<capability>`.\n\nTitle: the capability as a verb phrase, such as \"Save an item to the wishlist\".\n\nBody: the claims, in the claim format, 3 to 7 of them, and nothing else. Observable behavior only. Owners live in index.md, history in git, code locations in architecture/boundaries.md, and how it's built in architecture/.\n\nWritten by docs-map when a domain is mapped and by spec-ship when a spec ships, which copies the spec's claims without rewording and applies each Add, Change and Remove. Never edited by hand during a spec; a change to a claim is a Change in the spec that ships it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
